INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Cerebrovascular Disease - Ischemic Stroke','easy','mcq','A 65-year-old hypertensive patient develops sudden onset right arm and face weakness with expressive aphasia lasting 10 minutes then completely resolves. This is best classified as:','["A. Ischaemic stroke","B. Transient ischaemic attack (TIA)","C. Todd paralysis","D. Complex migraine"]','B','A TIA (transient ischaemic attack) produces focal neurological deficits that resolve completely within 24 hours (classically within 1 hour). It represents transient focal brain ischaemia without infarction. TIA is a medical emergency — 10-15% risk of stroke within 90 days (highest in first 48 hours). This patient has left MCA territory TIA.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Cerebrovascular Disease - Ischemic Stroke','easy','mcq','The penumbra in acute ischaemic stroke refers to which zone?','["A. The core of irreversibly infarcted tissue","B. The surrounding zone of ischaemic but potentially salvageable tissue that maintains electrical silence but retains membrane integrity","C. The zone of oedema surrounding the infarct","D. The area of reactive astrocytosis remote from the infarct"]','B','The ischaemic penumbra is the zone surrounding the infarct core where blood flow is reduced enough to impair function (electrical silence) but not enough to cause irreversible cell death (maintains ionic homeostasis). The penumbra is the target of thrombolysis (tPA) and mechanical thrombectomy — salvaging it limits disability.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Cerebrovascular Disease - Ischemic Stroke','medium','mcq','Which pathological finding is MOST characteristic of an ischaemic stroke that is 12-24 hours old on histology?','["A. Red neurons (eosinophilic cytoplasm, pyknotic nucleus)","B. Neutrophil infiltration","C. Foamy macrophage (lipid-laden microglia) accumulation","D. Reactive astrocytosis (gliosis)"]','A','Red neurons (ischaemic cell change) appear within 12-24 hours: eosinophilic cytoplasm, pyknotic/triangular nucleus, loss of Nissl substance. This represents irreversible ischaemic neuronal injury. Neutrophil infiltration peaks 48-72 hours; macrophages appear days 3-5; gliosis develops over weeks.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Cerebrovascular Disease - Ischemic Stroke','medium','mcq','Lacunar infarcts are small (<1.5 cm) subcortical infarcts caused by which mechanism?','["A. Cardioembolism from atrial fibrillation","B. Lipohyalinosis of small penetrating arteries due to chronic hypertension","C. Large artery atherosclerosis of the MCA","D. Venous sinus thrombosis"]','B','Lacunar infarcts result from lipohyalinosis (hyaline arteriolosclerosis) of small penetrating arteries (lenticulostriate arteries, thalamoperforators, basilar perforators) due to chronic hypertension. Classic locations: putamen, thalamus, pons, caudate, internal capsule. Present as pure motor, pure sensory, or ataxic hemiparesis syndromes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Cerebrovascular Disease - Ischemic Stroke','medium','mcq','Watershed (boundary zone) infarcts most commonly occur in which clinical scenario?','["A. Focal arterial occlusion by thrombus","B. Global hypoperfusion (systemic hypotension, cardiac arrest, severe hypovolaemia)","C. Hypertensive encephalopathy","D. Vasospasm after subarachnoid haemorrhage"]','B','Watershed infarcts occur in the boundary zones between major arterial territories (ACA/MCA and MCA/PCA junctions) during systemic hypoperfusion, when these distal zones are most vulnerable. Classic scenario: cardiac arrest, prolonged shock, carotid stenosis. The man-in-a-barrel syndrome (proximal arm weakness) occurs from bilateral ACA/MCA watershed infarcts.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Cerebrovascular Disease - Ischemic Stroke','medium','mcq','On MRI, diffusion-weighted imaging (DWI) in acute ischaemic stroke shows restricted diffusion because:','["A. Vasogenic oedema causes BBB breakdown and extracellular water accumulation","B. Cytotoxic oedema causes failure of Na+/K+ ATPase, cell swelling and reduced extracellular space restricting water diffusion","C. Haemosiderin deposits cause magnetic susceptibility changes","D. Gadolinium contrast does not penetrate the intact BBB"]','B','DWI detects CYTOTOXIC OEDEMA: ATP failure → Na+/K+ ATPase stops → Na+ and water enter cells → cells swell → extracellular space shrinks → water diffusion restricted → DWI bright/ADC dark. DWI is positive within minutes of stroke onset, making it the most sensitive early imaging for acute ischaemia.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Cerebrovascular Disease - Ischemic Stroke','medium','mcq','The most common cardioembolic cause of ischaemic stroke is:','["A. Mitral valve prolapse","B. Atrial fibrillation (AF)","C. Patent foramen ovale (PFO)","D. Left ventricular hypertrophy"]','B','Atrial fibrillation (AF) is the most common cardioembolic cause of stroke, responsible for 15-20% of all ischaemic strokes. AF causes stasis of blood in the left atrial appendage, forming thrombi that embolise to the cerebral circulation. AF-related strokes are large, cortical, and often haemorrhagically transform. Anticoagulation prevents recurrence.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Cerebrovascular Disease - Ischemic Stroke','hard','mcq','A 72-year-old with known carotid artery stenosis develops a large middle cerebral artery infarct. Histology at 3 weeks would show which PREDOMINANT pathological features?','["A. Red neurons with neutrophil infiltrate","B. Abundant foamy macrophages (lipid-laden, phagocytosing myelin) with angiogenesis and reactive astrocytes at the border","C. Established gliotic scar with no macrophages","D. Acute haemorrhage with haemosiderin-laden macrophages"]','B','At 3 weeks (subacute stage), the dominant feature is foamy macrophage (microglial/monocyte) infiltration phagocytosing necrotic tissue and myelin. Angiogenesis (new capillary formation) is active. Reactive astrocytes form at the border. By months, a glial scar (gliosis, no macrophages) replaces the necrotic tissue — unlike in other organs, there is no fibrosis in the CNS.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Cerebrovascular Disease - Ischemic Stroke','hard','mcq','Which area of the hippocampus is MOST vulnerable to global ischaemia (selective vulnerability)?','["A. CA2 region","B. CA1 region (Sommer sector)","C. CA3 region","D. Dentate gyrus"]','B','The CA1 region of the hippocampus (Sommer sector) is the most selectively vulnerable to global ischaemia (cardiac arrest). Other selectively vulnerable areas: Purkinje cells of the cerebellum, pyramidal neurons of layers III, V, VI of the neocortex, and striatal neurons. Selective vulnerability is due to high metabolic demand, high NMDA receptor density, and poor Ca2+ buffering.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Cerebrovascular Disease - Ischemic Stroke','easy','mcq','Which major modifiable risk factor is most strongly associated with BOTH ischaemic and haemorrhagic stroke?','["A. Hypercholesterolaemia","B. Hypertension","C. Obesity","D. Smoking"]','B','Hypertension is the single most important modifiable risk factor for both ischaemic and haemorrhagic stroke. It causes atherosclerosis of large and medium arteries (ischaemic stroke risk), lipohyalinosis of small perforating arteries (lacunar infarcts), and weakening of arterial walls (haemorrhagic stroke risk). Blood pressure control is the most effective stroke prevention intervention.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Cerebrovascular Disease - Haemorrhagic','easy','mcq','A 55-year-old hypertensive patient presents with sudden severe headache (''thunderclap''), meningism, and photophobia. CT shows blood in the basal cisterns. The most likely diagnosis is:','["A. Intracerebral haemorrhage (ICH)","B. Subarachnoid haemorrhage (SAH)","C. Subdural haematoma","D. Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis"]','B','SAH from a ruptured berry (saccular) aneurysm presents with the classic ''thunderclap headache'' — the worst headache of one''''s life, of sudden onset. Blood fills the subarachnoid space (basal cisterns on CT: hyperdense). Complications: rebleed (24-48 hrs), vasospasm (days 4-14), hydrocephalus, hyponatraemia. CT sensitivity: ~98% within 12 hours, then LP for xanthochromia.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Cerebrovascular Disease - Haemorrhagic','easy','mcq','Berry (saccular) aneurysms most commonly occur at which anatomical sites?','["A. Along the posterior cerebral artery","B. At arterial bifurcations in the circle of Willis, especially the anterior communicating artery (ACoA)","C. In the posterior inferior cerebellar artery","D. In the middle cerebral artery (M3-M4 segments)"]','B','Berry aneurysms occur at bifurcations in the circle of Willis, where haemodynamic stress is greatest and the media is congenitally weak (absent in the angle of bifurcation). Most common sites: ACoA junction (~30%), PCoA-ICA junction (~25%), MCA bifurcation (~20%). Multiple in 20-30% of cases. Associated with: ADPKD, coarctation of aorta, Ehlers-Danlos, Marfan syndrome.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Cerebrovascular Disease - Haemorrhagic','medium','mcq','Hypertensive intracerebral haemorrhage (ICH) most commonly occurs in which location?','["A. Frontal lobe cortex","B. Putamen/basal ganglia (supplied by lenticulostriate arteries)","C. Cerebellum vermis","D. Corpus callosum"]','B','Hypertensive ICH most commonly occurs in the putamen/basal ganglia (~50%), supplied by the lenticulostriate arteries — small perforating vessels prone to Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysm formation from chronic hypertension. Other common sites: thalamus, pons, cerebellum (lobar haemorrhages suggest amyloid angiopathy, AVM, or tumour).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Cerebrovascular Disease - Haemorrhagic','medium','mcq','Cerebral amyloid angiopathy (CAA) is an important cause of lobar intracerebral haemorrhage in elderly patients. Which protein is deposited in the vessel walls?','["A. Transthyretin (TTR)","B. Amyloid-beta (Abeta), particularly Abeta40","C. Alpha-synuclein","D. TDP-43"]','B','CAA involves deposition of amyloid-beta (predominantly Abeta40) in the walls of cortical and leptomeningeal small arteries, leading to vessel fragility and risk of lobar haemorrhage. CAA is common in elderly patients and those with Alzheimer disease. Diagnosis: MRI gradient echo/SWI showing multiple haemosiderin deposits in the cortex (cortical microbleeds, cortical superficial siderosis).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Cerebrovascular Disease - Haemorrhagic','medium','mcq','A 30-year-old presents with sudden headache during exercise and is found to have a cerebral arteriovenous malformation (AVM) that bled. AVMs are characterised pathologically by:','["A. Saccular dilatation at an arterial bifurcation with absent tunica media","B. Abnormal direct connections between arteries and veins without a normal capillary bed, with abnormally enlarged, thin-walled vessels","C. Intimal proliferation and lipid deposits in large cerebral arteries","D. Venous thrombosis within a dural sinus"]','B','AVMs are congenital vascular malformations consisting of abnormal direct arteriovenous connections (shunts) without a normal intervening capillary bed. The tangled nidus of vessels has abnormally thin walls prone to rupture. Annual bleed risk: ~2-4%. Associated: Osler-Weber-Rendu (HHT). MRI shows flow voids; angiography is gold standard.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Cerebrovascular Disease - Haemorrhagic','medium','mcq','A subdural haematoma (SDH) results from rupture of which vessels?','["A. Middle meningeal artery","B. Bridging veins traversing the subdural space","C. Cortical bridging arteries","D. Dural venous sinuses"]','B','Acute SDH results from rupture of bridging veins that traverse the subdural space from the cortex to the dural sinuses. These veins are susceptible to tearing with acceleration-deceleration head injury (in elderly patients, even trivial trauma). Blood accumulates between the dura and arachnoid. Note: epidural haematoma (EDH) = middle meningeal artery, biconvex hyperdensity, lucid interval.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Cerebrovascular Disease - Haemorrhagic','medium','mcq','The complication of vasospasm after subarachnoid haemorrhage typically occurs at which time period?','["A. Within the first 24 hours","B. Days 4-14 post-SAH","C. Day 21-28 post-SAH","D. Only after surgical clipping of the aneurysm"]','B','Vasospasm after SAH is a feared complication causing delayed cerebral ischaemia (DCI), occurring typically days 4-14 post-SAH (peak at day 7-10). Mechanism: breakdown products of subarachnoid blood (oxyhaemoglobin, endothelin) cause arterial spasm. Prevention: nimodipine (calcium channel blocker). Monitoring: transcranial Doppler. Treatment: triple-H therapy (hypertension, hypervolaemia, haemodilution).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Cerebrovascular Disease - Haemorrhagic','hard','mcq','A Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysm is most closely associated with which pathological process?','["A. Atherosclerosis of the circle of Willis","B. Chronic hypertension causing segmental arteriolar dilatation of small perforating arteries","C. Berry aneurysm formation at bifurcations","D. Inflammatory vasculitis of cerebral vessels"]','B','Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms are small (< 2 mm) aneurysms in small penetrating cerebral arteries (putamen, thalamus, pons, cerebellum) caused by chronic hypertension and lipohyalinosis. They are distinct from berry aneurysms (bifurcation, SAH risk). Charcot-Bouchard rupture causes deep hypertensive ICH. First described in the 19th century by Charcot and Bouchard.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Cerebrovascular Disease - Haemorrhagic','hard','mcq','A patient with SAH develops hyponatraemia on day 5. The MOST likely cause is:','["A. Inappropriate secretion of ADH from hypothalamic compression (SIADH)","B. Cerebral salt wasting (CSW) — natriuresis driven by BNP/ANP release from the injured brain","C. Adrenal insufficiency from pituitary stalk compression","D. Excessive water intake (psychogenic polydipsia)"]','B','In SAH, the most common cause of hyponatraemia is cerebral salt wasting (CSW): the injured brain releases BNP (brain natriuretic peptide) causing massive renal sodium and water loss, volume depletion, and hyponatraemia. Treatment: sodium replacement and volume repletion. SIADH (euvolaemic/hypervolaemic, restrict fluids) must be distinguished from CSW (hypovolaemic, give fluids+salt) — crucial because fluid restriction in CSW worsens vasospasm.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Cerebrovascular Disease - Haemorrhagic','easy','mcq','Which feature on CT best differentiates an acute epidural haematoma (EDH) from a subdural haematoma (SDH)?','["A. Location on the same side as the impact (coup)","B. Biconvex (lens-shaped) hyperdense collection NOT crossing suture lines (EDH) vs. crescent-shaped collection crossing sutures (SDH)","C. Presence of midline shift","D. Associated skull fracture"]','B','EDH: biconvex (lens-shaped) hyperdensity on CT, does NOT cross suture lines (because the periosteum is fused at sutures), typically from middle meningeal artery rupture (temporal bone fracture). SDH: crescent-shaped hyperdensity, can cross suture lines (but cannot cross the midline or tentorium). EDH classic presentation: lucid interval then rapid deterioration.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Infections','easy','mcq','A 25-year-old presents with fever, headache, photophobia, neck stiffness, and a petechial/purpuric rash. CSF shows: turbid, neutrophils 2000 cells/mm3, protein 200 mg/dL, glucose 1.1 mmol/L (blood glucose 5.5 mmol/L). The most likely organism is:','["A. Streptococcus pneumoniae","B. Neisseria meningitidis","C. Haemophilus influenzae","D. Listeria monocytogenes"]','B','The purpuric/petechial rash is PATHOGNOMONIC of meningococcal meningitis (Neisseria meningitidis, gram-negative diplococcus). N. meningitidis is the most common cause in adolescents/young adults and causes epidemics. It can progress to Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome (bilateral adrenal haemorrhage, septic shock). Treatment: immediate benzylpenicillin/ceftriaxone. The rash is non-blanching.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Infections','easy','mcq','The most common cause of bacterial meningitis in adults (>18 years) overall is:','["A. Neisseria meningitidis","B. Streptococcus pneumoniae","C. Listeria monocytogenes","D. Group B Streptococcus"]','B','Streptococcus pneumoniae (pneumococcus) is the most common cause of community-acquired bacterial meningitis in adults >18 years. It is a gram-positive diplococcus. Risk factors: splenectomy/asplenia (encapsulated organisms), sickle cell disease, CSF leak, cochlear implants. High mortality (~20-30%). Treatment: ceftriaxone + vancomycin + dexamethasone (reduce inflammation).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Infections','medium','mcq','CSF findings in viral (aseptic) meningitis differ from bacterial meningitis in that viral meningitis shows:','["A. Neutrophilic pleocytosis with very low glucose","B. Lymphocytic pleocytosis with normal or mildly reduced glucose and normal or mildly elevated protein","C. Xanthochromia with many RBCs","D. Completely normal CSF"]','B','Viral meningitis CSF: lymphocytic pleocytosis (10-500 cells/mm3), normal or mildly reduced glucose (>50% serum glucose), normal or mildly elevated protein (<100 mg/dL). Causative organisms: enteroviruses (most common), HSV-2, mumps, HIV. Contrast with bacterial: neutrophilia, very low glucose, high protein, turbid appearance.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Infections','medium','mcq','Brain abscess most commonly arises from which source of infection?','["A. Haematogenous spread from distant sites","B. Contiguous spread from adjacent structures (sinusitis, otitis media, mastoiditis) OR haematogenous from distant sites","C. Direct inoculation from head trauma","D. Reactivation of latent viral infection"]','B','Brain abscess sources: (1) Contiguous spread: frontal abscess from sinusitis (Streptococcus, anaerobes); temporal/cerebellar abscess from otitis/mastoiditis (mixed flora). (2) Haematogenous: from pulmonary infections, endocarditis, dental infections — multiple abscesses, often at grey-white junction. (3) Trauma/post-surgical. Commonest organisms: Streptococcus viridans group, anaerobes, Staphylococcus. CT: ring-enhancing lesion with oedema.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Infections','medium','mcq','Tuberculous meningitis characteristically shows which CSF findings?','["A. Neutrophilic pleocytosis, very low glucose, gram-positive cocci on stain","B. Lymphocytic pleocytosis, very low glucose, high protein (cobweb clot on standing), AFB on smear (low sensitivity)","C. Eosinophilic pleocytosis, normal glucose, low protein","D. Normal CSF with positive blood cultures"]','B','TB meningitis CSF: lymphocytic pleocytosis (100-500 cells/mm3), VERY LOW glucose (<2.2 mmol/L), HIGH protein (>100 mg/dL, may form a cobweb clot on standing). AFB smear has low sensitivity (~30%) but specificity is high. PCR and GeneXpert increase sensitivity. Pathology: granulomatous inflammation at the base of brain (basilar meningitis) causing cranial nerve palsies and vasculitis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Infections','medium','mcq','Herpes simplex encephalitis (HSE) most commonly affects which brain regions and what is the treatment?','["A. Brainstem (CN nuclei) — treatment: acyclovir","B. Temporal and frontal lobes (limbic encephalitis pattern) — treatment: IV acyclovir","C. Cerebellum — treatment: corticosteroids","D. Basal ganglia — treatment: antifungals"]','B','HSE (mostly HSV-1 in adults) has tropism for the TEMPORAL LOBES and ORBITOFRONTAL CORTEX (limbic structures). Presents: fever, personality change, amnesia, seizures, temporal lobe signs. MRI: T2/FLAIR signal in temporal lobes. EEG: periodic lateralised epileptiform discharges (PLEDs) from temporal lobes. Treatment: IV acyclovir (immediately on suspicion). Untreated: ~70% mortality. CSF: lymphocytic pleocytosis, HSV PCR positive.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Infections','medium','mcq','A 35-year-old HIV-positive patient with CD4 count 30 cells/microL develops subacute meningitis with raised ICP and headache. India ink stain of CSF shows encapsulated yeast cells. The diagnosis is:','["A. Toxoplasma encephalitis","B. Cryptococcal meningitis (Cryptococcus neoformans)","C. CNS lymphoma","D. PML (JC virus)"]','B','Cryptococcal meningitis (Cryptococcus neoformans): occurs in severely immunocompromised patients (HIV CD4 <100, transplant). India ink stain: encapsulated yeasts (capsule appears as a clear halo). CSF: lymphocytic/minimal pleocytosis, high opening pressure, very few cells (minimal inflammatory response), positive cryptococcal antigen (highly sensitive). Treatment: Amphotericin B + flucytosine (induction), then fluconazole (maintenance).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Infections','hard','mcq','Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML) is caused by which pathogen and affects which cells?','["A. HSV reactivating in the temporal lobe neurons","B. JC virus (John Cunningham virus, a polyomavirus) reactivating in oligodendrocytes, causing demyelination","C. CMV reactivating in ependymal cells causing ventriculitis","D. Toxoplasma gondii infecting astrocytes"]','B','PML is caused by JC virus (a polyomavirus), a ubiquitous virus (seroprevalence ~50-70%) that reactivates in immunocompromised patients (HIV with CD4 <200, natalizumab treatment, chemotherapy). JC virus infects and destroys OLIGODENDROCYTES, causing multifocal demyelination. MRI: multiple white matter lesions without mass effect or enhancement. No specific antiviral treatment; immune reconstitution (ART) is key.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Infections','hard','mcq','Listeria monocytogenes meningitis occurs predominantly in which patient populations?','["A. Adolescents and young adults in close-contact settings","B. Neonates (<1 month), elderly (>60 years), pregnant women, and immunocompromised patients","C. Children 6 months to 5 years","D. Patients with splenectomy or functional asplenia"]','B','Listeria monocytogenes: gram-positive rod, causes meningitis and rhombencephalitis (brainstem involvement). Classic high-risk groups: neonates (vertical transmission), elderly, pregnant women (mild flu-like illness in mother, severe neonatal disease), T-cell immunodeficient patients (HIV, organ transplant, corticosteroids). Key: it is resistant to cephalosporins — empirical treatment includes AMPICILLIN for at-risk groups.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Infections','easy','mcq','The Kernig and Brudzinski signs are clinical signs of which condition?','["A. Raised intracranial pressure","B. Meningism (meningeal irritation)","C. Cerebellar herniation","D. Encephalitis"]','B','Kernig sign: with hip flexed to 90 degrees, extension of the knee is painful (>135 degrees). Brudzinski sign: passive neck flexion causes involuntary hip and knee flexion. Both indicate MENINGISM — irritation of the meninges (meningitis, SAH). They are caused by stretching of inflamed meninges and nerve roots. Both have modest sensitivity (~50%) but high specificity when positive.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Demyelinating Diseases','easy','mcq','Multiple sclerosis (MS) plaques (demyelinating lesions) are characteristically found in which CNS locations?','["A. Anterior horn of the spinal cord","B. Periventricular white matter, juxtacortical, brainstem, cerebellum, and spinal cord; especially at the angles of the lateral ventricles (Dawson fingers)","C. Grey matter of the basal ganglia","D. Peripheral nerves only"]','B','MS plaques are characteristically in the white matter, with predilection for: periventricular (Dawson fingers — perpendicular to lateral ventricles on sagittal MRI), juxtacortical, infratentorial (posterior fossa, brainstem, cerebellum), and spinal cord. These correspond to the McDonald criteria dissemination in space. The lesions spare the peripheral nervous system (axons are preserved initially, myelin is lost).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Demyelinating Diseases','easy','mcq','Oligoclonal bands (OCBs) in CSF are found in the majority of MS patients. They represent:','["A. Intrathecal immunoglobulin G production indicating CNS inflammation","B. Myelin basic protein released from damaged myelin sheaths","C. Antibodies against aquaporin-4","D. Elevated albumin due to BBB breakdown"]','B','OCBs are bands of IgG on CSF electrophoresis (isoelectric focusing) that are PRESENT IN CSF but NOT in serum — indicating INTRATHECAL IgG synthesis. Present in >90% of MS patients. Also seen in: CNS infections (syphilis, Lyme), neurosarcoidosis, and other inflammatory CNS diseases. Note: OCBs are not specific for MS but support the diagnosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Demyelinating Diseases','medium','mcq','A young woman presents with recurrent episodes of unilateral optic neuritis and complete bilateral ascending paralysis with sensory loss up to the cervical level. MRI shows longitudinally extensive transverse myelitis (LETM, >3 vertebral segments). CSF shows no OCBs. The most likely diagnosis is:','["A. Multiple sclerosis (MS)","B. Neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder (NMOSD) — aquaporin-4 antibody positive","C. Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis (ADEM)","D. Spinal cord infarction"]','B','NMOSD is an astrocytopathy caused by IgG antibodies against aquaporin-4 (AQP4) water channels on astrocyte endfeet. Characteristic: optic neuritis (bilateral or rapidly sequential, severe, often does not recover well) + LETM (>3 spinal segments, complete myelitis). MRI: spinal cord signal extending >3 segments. CSF: no OCBs (in contrast to MS). Treatment: steroids acutely, then mycophenolate/rituximab.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Demyelinating Diseases','medium','mcq','Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis (ADEM) is characterised by which feature that distinguishes it from MS?','["A. It only occurs in adults","B. It is a monophasic, multifocal demyelinating disorder typically following infection or vaccination with widespread grey and white matter involvement and encephalopathy","C. It always has oligoclonal bands in CSF","D. It predominantly affects the spinal cord causing paraplegia"]','B','ADEM: monophasic (one episode) post-infectious or post-vaccination demyelinating disease, predominantly in children. Presentation: encephalopathy + multifocal neurological deficits (optic neuritis, myelitis, brainstem signs). MRI: large, bilateral, asymmetric white matter lesions (often also involving grey matter). No OCBs. Treatment: high-dose IV methylprednisolone. Most recover well. Recurrent ADEM raises suspicion for NMOSD (especially MOG-IgG) or MS.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Demyelinating Diseases','medium','mcq','The acute MS plaque histologically shows:','["A. Amyloid plaques and neurofibrillary tangles","B. Perivascular T-cell and macrophage infiltrate with loss of myelin but relative preservation of axons, and oligodendrocyte loss","C. Wallerian degeneration of axons throughout the white matter","D. Lewy body accumulation in oligodendrocytes"]','B','Active MS plaques: perivenous T lymphocytes (CD4+ and CD8+) and activated macrophages phagocytosing myelin. Loss of myelin (stain negative with Luxol fast blue), but RELATIVE PRESERVATION of axons (early disease). Oligodendrocytes are destroyed, contributing to failed remyelination. Shadow plaques (remyelinated) show thin myelin sheaths. In progressive MS, axonal loss becomes prominent.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Demyelinating Diseases','medium','mcq','A patient with MS most likely has which type of MS as the initial presentation?','["A. Primary progressive MS (PPMS)","B. Relapsing-remitting MS (RRMS)","C. Secondary progressive MS (SPMS)","D. Progressive relapsing MS"]','B','~85% of MS patients present with relapsing-remitting MS (RRMS): discrete episodes of neurological dysfunction (relapses) followed by partial or complete recovery (remissions). Over time (~50% at 10 years), RRMS converts to secondary progressive MS (SPMS) — accumulating disability without discrete relapses. Primary progressive MS (PPMS, ~15%) shows continuous decline from onset (older males, spinal cord predominant).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Demyelinating Diseases','medium','mcq','The most common symptom of MS in clinical practice is:','["A. Epileptic seizures","B. Fatigue","C. Psychosis","D. Parkinsonism"]','B','Fatigue is the most common and disabling symptom of MS, affecting ~80% of patients. Other common features: Uhthoff phenomenon (worsening with heat/exercise), optic neuritis (painful visual loss), INO (bilateral = MS until proved otherwise), Lhermitte sign (electric shock down the spine on neck flexion), bladder dysfunction, spasticity. Seizures occur but are uncommon.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Demyelinating Diseases','hard','mcq','The immunopathogenesis of MS is best described as:','["A. B-cell mediated autoimmune attack on peripheral myelin (Schwann cells) via anti-GM1 antibodies","B. T-cell mediated autoimmune attack on CNS myelin, with CD4+ Th1/Th17 cells crossing the BBB, activating macrophages and CD8+ cytotoxic T cells to destroy oligodendrocytes and myelin","C. Complement-mediated astrocyte destruction via anti-AQP4 antibodies","D. Viral cytopathic effect on neurons via reactivation of HHV-6"]','B','MS immunopathogenesis: autoreactive CD4+ T cells (Th1 producing IFN-gamma, and Th17 producing IL-17) are activated in the periphery (possibly by molecular mimicry with myelin antigens), cross the BBB, and trigger inflammatory cascades. CD8+ T cells directly kill oligodendrocytes/neurons. Macrophages phagocytose myelin. B cells and antibodies contribute in some MS subtypes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Demyelinating Diseases','hard','mcq','Which MRI criterion must be met for dissemination in TIME (DIT) in the diagnosis of MS according to McDonald criteria (2017)?','["A. Lesions in at least 2 of 4 locations: periventricular, juxtacortical, infratentorial, spinal cord","B. A new T2 lesion on follow-up MRI compared to a baseline scan, OR simultaneous presence of asymptomatic gadolinium-enhancing AND non-enhancing lesions on a single scan","C. Oligoclonal bands in CSF","D. At least 3 gadolinium-enhancing lesions on a single scan"]','B','McDonald 2017 DIT criteria: (1) A new T2 or gadolinium-enhancing lesion on follow-up MRI (compared to a baseline scan taken at any time), OR (2) Simultaneous presence of asymptomatic gadolinium-enhancing and non-enhancing lesions at any time. CSF OCBs can substitute for DIT. DIS (dissemination in space): lesions in >=2 of 4 locations (periventricular, juxtacortical/cortical, infratentorial, spinal cord).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Demyelinating Diseases','easy','mcq','The Lhermitte sign, an electric shock-like sensation running down the spine on neck flexion, is most commonly associated with:','["A. Cervical spondylotic myelopathy","B. Demyelination of the posterior columns of the cervical spinal cord (e.g., MS)","C. Herniated cervical disc compressing the nerve root","D. Subacute combined degeneration (vitamin B12 deficiency)"]','B','Lhermitte sign: electric shock down the spine and limbs on neck flexion. Caused by mechanical sensitisation of demyelinated axons in the cervical dorsal columns. Classic in MS but also in: cervical spondylotic myelopathy, B12 deficiency, radiation myelopathy. Not pathognomonic of MS but very suggestive in the right clinical context.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Neurodegenerative Diseases I','easy','mcq','The pathological hallmarks of Alzheimer disease (AD) are:','["A. Lewy bodies and Lewy neurites","B. Senile (neuritic) plaques (extracellular amyloid-beta) and neurofibrillary tangles (NFTs, intracellular hyperphosphorylated tau)","C. Prion protein deposits (PrPsc) in cortical neurons","D. Polyglutamine aggregates in striatal neurons"]','B','AD hallmarks: (1) Senile plaques: extracellular deposits of amyloid-beta (Abeta42), with dystrophic neurites and activated microglia/astrocytes. (2) Neurofibrillary tangles (NFTs): intracellular accumulations of hyperphosphorylated tau protein forming paired helical filaments. Pattern of spread: Braak staging I-VI (entorhinal cortex → hippocampus → association cortex → primary cortex).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Neurodegenerative Diseases I','easy','mcq','The most important genetic risk factor for late-onset sporadic Alzheimer disease is:','["A. Presenilin-1 (PSEN1) mutation","B. Amyloid precursor protein (APP) mutation","C. Apolipoprotein E epsilon4 (APOE4) allele","D. LRRK2 mutation"]','B','APOE4 (apolipoprotein E epsilon4) is the major genetic risk factor for LATE-ONSET SPORADIC AD. APOE4 increases Abeta clearance impairment and amyloid deposition. One APOE4 copy: ~3x risk. Two copies: ~8-12x risk. PSEN1 mutations: early-onset familial AD (most common cause of early-onset familial AD, chromosome 14). APP mutations: chromosome 21 (Down syndrome patients almost universally develop AD pathology by 50s).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Neurodegenerative Diseases I','medium','mcq','In Alzheimer disease, the pattern of amyloid plaque deposition spreads from which area FIRST?','["A. Primary motor cortex","B. Neocortex (association areas) first, then allocortex, then subcortical","C. Entorhinal cortex and hippocampus (CA1, subiculum)","D. Cerebellum and brainstem"]','C','Amyloid deposition (Thal phases): Phase 1: neocortex (particularly associative areas). Phase 2: entorhinal/hippocampus. Phase 3: striatum. Phase 4: brainstem. Phase 5: cerebellum. NFT/tau (Braak staging): begins in entorhinal cortex/hippocampus (stages I-II), spreads to limbic system (III-IV), then neocortex (V-VI). Memory symptoms correlate best with NFT burden in the hippocampus.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Neurodegenerative Diseases I','medium','mcq','Lewy bodies, the pathological hallmark of Parkinson disease, are composed primarily of which protein?','["A. Hyperphosphorylated tau","B. Amyloid-beta 42","C. Alpha-synuclein (aggregated, ubiquitinated)","D. TDP-43"]','B','Lewy bodies are intracytoplasmic eosinophilic inclusions composed of aggregated, ubiquitinated alpha-synuclein. They appear as round, pink, glassy inclusions with a pale halo in neurons. Braak staging of Lewy body pathology begins in the dorsal motor nucleus of CN X and olfactory bulb (stage 1-2), spreading to substantia nigra (stage 3-4) and cortex (stage 5-6). Parkinson disease is an alpha-synucleinopathy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Neurodegenerative Diseases I','medium','mcq','A 65-year-old man presents with resting tremor (pill-rolling), rigidity, bradykinesia, and festinant gait with no dementia. He responds well to levodopa. This is most consistent with:','["A. Multiple system atrophy (MSA)","B. Progressive supranuclear palsy (PSP)","C. Idiopathic Parkinson disease","D. Dementia with Lewy bodies (DLB)"]','C','Idiopathic Parkinson disease (IPD): asymmetric onset, rest tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia, postural instability (TRAP). Classic pathology: Lewy bodies in substantia nigra pars compacta, loss of dopaminergic neurons causing dopamine deficiency in the striatum. Excellent levodopa response is characteristic of IPD (in contrast to atypical parkinsonian syndromes: MSA, PSP, CBD which have poor response).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Neurodegenerative Diseases I','medium','mcq','Substantia nigra neuronal loss in Parkinson disease leads to impaired motor function primarily through which mechanism?','["A. Direct degeneration of motor neurons in the anterior horn","B. Loss of dopaminergic nigrostriatal neurons causing dopamine deficiency in the striatum, leading to imbalanced basal ganglia output (overactive indirect pathway)","C. Demyelination of the corticospinal tract","D. Loss of cholinergic neurons in the nucleus basalis of Meynert"]','B','SNpc dopaminergic neurons project via the nigrostriatal pathway to the striatum. Dopamine depletion (>80% neuronal loss before symptoms appear): D1 receptor stimulation is reduced (direct pathway impaired) and D2 receptor inhibition is reduced (indirect pathway overactive). Net effect: increased GPi/SNpr activity → thalamic inhibition → reduced cortical motor activation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Neurodegenerative Diseases I','medium','mcq','Which gross brain pathology finding is characteristic of Alzheimer disease at autopsy?','["A. Hydrocephalus ex vacuo with atrophied periventricular white matter","B. Frontoparietal cortical atrophy with widened sulci and narrowed gyri, particularly in the temporal and parietal lobes, with hippocampal atrophy","C. Atrophy of the caudate nucleus with enlarged lateral ventricles","D. Atrophy of the cerebellum with loss of Purkinje cells"]','B','AD gross pathology: cortical atrophy (particularly temporal and parietal lobes and frontal association areas), widened sulci, narrowed gyri, hippocampal atrophy, and ex vacuo hydrocephalus. The primary motor cortex and primary sensory cortex are relatively spared (explaining preserved limb function until late stages). The amygdala and parahippocampal gyrus are also severely atrophied.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Neurodegenerative Diseases I','hard','mcq','The amyloid cascade hypothesis of Alzheimer disease proposes that:','["A. Tau hyperphosphorylation is the initiating event, causing Abeta aggregation secondarily","B. Accumulation of Abeta42 (from APP processing by beta- and gamma-secretase) is the initiating event, triggering tau hyperphosphorylation, synaptic loss, neuroinflammation, and neuronal death","C. Cholinergic deficit in the nucleus basalis of Meynert initiates the cascade","D. ApoE4 directly phosphorylates tau causing NFT formation"]','B','The amyloid cascade hypothesis (Hardy and Higgins, 1992): APP is cleaved by beta-secretase (BACE1) then gamma-secretase (presenilin complex) to generate Abeta42, which aggregates into oligomers, fibrils, and senile plaques. Abeta oligomers cause synaptic dysfunction, mitochondrial failure, and trigger tau hyperphosphorylation and NFT formation. This leads to neuronal death. Evidence: familial AD mutations all increase Abeta42 production or aggregation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Neurodegenerative Diseases I','hard','mcq','Which enzyme activity is used as a biomarker for Alzheimer disease in CSF, and what is the pattern of change?','["A. Decreased acetylcholinesterase activity (reflecting cholinergic loss)","B. Decreased CSF Abeta42 (amyloid sequestered in plaques) and increased CSF total tau and phospho-tau (tau released from dying neurons)","C. Increased CSF 14-3-3 protein (reflecting rapid neuronal loss)","D. Decreased CSF neurofilament light chain (NfL)"]','B','AD CSF biomarkers: (1) LOW Abeta42: amyloid is sequestered in plaques, so less enters CSF. (2) HIGH total tau (t-tau): reflects neuronal/axonal damage and death. (3) HIGH phospho-tau (p-tau, especially p-tau181, p-tau217): reflects NFT formation and is more specific for AD than t-tau alone. The ratio of Abeta42/Abeta40 is also used. These CSF changes precede clinical symptoms by 10-15 years.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Neurodegenerative Diseases I','easy','mcq','The cholinergic hypothesis of Alzheimer disease is supported by loss of neurons in which structure?','["A. Locus coeruleus","B. Nucleus basalis of Meynert (basal forebrain)","C. Raphe nuclei","D. Caudate nucleus"]','B','The cholinergic hypothesis: AD patients have severe loss of acetylcholinergic neurons in the nucleus basalis of Meynert (basal forebrain), which projects to the hippocampus and cortex. This loss correlates with cognitive impairment. Basis for cholinesterase inhibitor treatment (donepezil, rivastigmine, galantamine) — increase ACh availability by inhibiting AChE.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Neurodegenerative Diseases II','easy','mcq','Huntington disease is caused by which type of mutation, and on which chromosome?','["A. CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the HTT gene on chromosome 4","B. CTG repeat expansion in the DMPK gene on chromosome 19","C. GAA repeat expansion in the FXN gene on chromosome 9","D. CGG repeat expansion in the FMR1 gene on chromosome X"]','A','HD: autosomal dominant, CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion (>36 repeats, normal <26) in the huntingtin (HTT) gene on chromosome 4p. Anticipation (earlier onset/more severe with each generation, especially paternal transmission). Pathological protein: mutant huntingtin (mHtt) with polyglutamine expansion, which forms nuclear and cytoplasmic aggregates and causes selective striatal neuronal death.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Neurodegenerative Diseases II','easy','mcq','Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) classically presents with a combination of which neurological signs?','["A. Pure UMN signs (spasticity and hyperreflexia only)","B. Pure LMN signs (flaccidity and atrophy only)","C. BOTH UMN signs (spasticity, hyperreflexia) AND LMN signs (fasciculations, atrophy) at the same or different levels","D. Cerebellar signs with UMN involvement"]','C','ALS (Lou Gehrig disease): degeneration of BOTH upper motor neurons (motor cortex → UMN signs: spasticity, hyperreflexia, Babinski, emotional lability) AND lower motor neurons (anterior horn cells → LMN signs: fasciculations, muscle wasting, weakness, hyporeflexia, cramps). The co-existence of UMN+LMN signs is the clinical hallmark. Bulbar involvement: dysphagia, dysarthria, tongue fasciculations. Median survival: 2-5 years from symptom onset.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Neurodegenerative Diseases II','medium','mcq','Which genetic mutation accounts for the majority of familial ALS cases and is also found in some sporadic cases?','["A. SOD1 (superoxide dismutase 1) mutation on chromosome 21","B. C9orf72 hexanucleotide repeat expansion (GGGGCC) — most common familial and sporadic ALS","C. TDP-43 (TARDBP) mutation","D. FUS mutation"]','B','C9orf72 GGGGCC hexanucleotide repeat expansion is now the most common genetic cause of both familial ALS (~40%) and sporadic ALS (~5-10%). It also causes familial frontotemporal dementia (FTD). Previously, SOD1 was considered most common (familial ALS ~20%). TDP-43 cytoplasmic inclusions are the pathological hallmark of most ALS cases (including C9orf72-ALS). 2011 landmark discovery.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Neurodegenerative Diseases II','medium','mcq','Kuru and Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease (CJD) are prion diseases. What is a prion?','["A. A small RNA virus that causes encephalitis","B. A misfolded conformational isoform of the normal cellular prion protein (PrPc) that is protease-resistant (PrPsc) and acts as a template for converting normal PrPc","C. A retrovirus that integrates into neuronal DNA causing progressive neurodegeneration","D. An autoimmune antibody attacking neuronal surface receptors"]','B','Prions (Prusiner, Nobel Prize 1997): proteinaceous infectious particles with no nucleic acid. The infectious agent is a misfolded isoform (PrPsc) of the normal cellular protein (PrPc). PrPsc acts as a template, inducing normal PrPc to misfold into PrPsc — autocatalytic propagation. PrPsc is protease-resistant, insoluble, and accumulates in spongiform vacuoles. CJD: rapidly progressive dementia, myoclonus, periodic sharp waves on EEG.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Neurodegenerative Diseases II','medium','mcq','Gross and microscopic pathological findings in Huntington disease include:','["A. Cerebellar atrophy with Purkinje cell loss","B. Bilateral caudate nucleus atrophy (boxcar ventricles on imaging) with striatal medium spiny neuron loss","C. Hippocampal atrophy with NFT accumulation","D. Frontal lobe atrophy with Pick bodies"]','B','HD pathology: Bilateral caudate nucleus atrophy (and putamen later) causing the classic boxcar/box-shaped lateral ventricles on CT/MRI. Histology: selective loss of medium spiny neurons (GABA-enkephalin+ and GABA-substance P+) in the striatum. Surviving neurons contain nuclear inclusions of mutant huntingtin (mHtt). Eventually, cortical atrophy. At autopsy, the brain may lose up to 40% of its weight.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Neurodegenerative Diseases II','medium','mcq','Fatal familial insomnia (FFI) is a prion disease characterised by:','["A. Rapidly progressive cerebellar ataxia with cerebellar cortical prion deposits","B. Progressive intractable insomnia, autonomic dysfunction, and ataxia with thalamic degeneration (dorsomedial and anterior thalamic nuclei)","C. Dementia with cortical spongiform change and periodic sharp wave complexes on EEG","D. Huntingtin CAG repeat expansion causing striatal degeneration"]','B','FFI: autosomal dominant prion disease (mutation at codon 178 of PRNP gene + methionine at codon 129). Presents with progressive INSOMNIA (complete), autonomic dysfunction (hyperhidrosis, tachycardia, hyperthermia, hypertension), cognitive decline, and motor signs. Pathology: severe thalamic degeneration (dorsomedial and anterior nuclei) with minimal spongiform change. The thalamus is the primary sleep-regulating structure.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Neurodegenerative Diseases II','medium','mcq','Variant CJD (vCJD), linked to consumption of BSE-contaminated beef, differs from sporadic CJD in which way?','["A. vCJD affects older patients (>65 years) and has a shorter disease course","B. vCJD affects younger patients (<50 years), has a psychiatric onset, longer disease course (13 months), and shows florid plaques (amyloid plaques surrounded by spongiform vacuoles) on neuropathology","C. vCJD has no prion protein on Western blot","D. vCJD only affects immunocompromised patients"]','B','vCJD (variant CJD, linked to BSE): Young patients (<40 years, mean ~28 years). Psychiatric onset (depression, anxiety, personality change), then cerebellar ataxia, then dementia. Longer disease course (~14 months vs. sporadic CJD average 4-6 months). Neuropathology: florid plaques (PrPsc amyloid cores surrounded by a ring of spongiform vacuoles) — pathognomonic. 14-3-3 protein elevated in CSF in both types.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Neurodegenerative Diseases II','hard','mcq','TDP-43 (TAR DNA-binding protein 43) pathology is found in the majority of ALS cases. What is the normal function of TDP-43 and what happens in disease?','["A. TDP-43 is normally a cytoplasmic kinase; in ALS it mislocalises to the nucleus","B. TDP-43 is normally a nuclear RNA-binding protein; in ALS it mislocalises to the cytoplasm, forms insoluble aggregates, and is depleted from the nucleus, impairing RNA splicing and causing neuronal death","C. TDP-43 is normally a motor protein; in ALS it accumulates in mitochondria","D. TDP-43 is normally part of the proteasome; in ALS it inhibits ubiquitin-mediated protein degradation"]','B','TDP-43 (nuclear RNA-binding protein) normally regulates RNA splicing, stability, and transport. In ALS (and FTD): TDP-43 mislocalises from the nucleus to the cytoplasm, forms phosphorylated, ubiquitinated cytoplasmic inclusions, and is depleted from the nucleus (loss of nuclear function). Both gain-of-toxic-function (cytoplasmic aggregates) and loss-of-function (nuclear depletion) contribute to neurodegeneration.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Neurodegenerative Diseases II','hard','mcq','In Huntington disease, which mechanism best explains the selective vulnerability of striatal medium spiny neurons despite ubiquitous mutant huntingtin expression?','["A. The striatum has the lowest levels of BDNF (brain-derived neurotrophic factor) compared to other brain regions","B. Striatal medium spiny neurons have high NMDA receptor activity, reduced BDNF (from cortical input loss), mitochondrial dysfunction, and impaired proteasomal function — all converging on selective excitotoxic death","C. The striatum lacks antioxidant defences (SOD, catalase)","D. Mutant huntingtin binds specifically to a striatal-enriched protein"]','B','Selective striatal vulnerability in HD involves multiple converging mechanisms: (1) High NMDA receptor density causing excitotoxicity. (2) Reduced cortical BDNF delivery (mHtt impairs BDNF transcription in cortex and axonal transport). (3) Mitochondrial dysfunction (mHtt disrupts ETC complex II/III). (4) Impaired proteasomal and autophagic protein clearance. (5) Transcriptional dysregulation (mHtt sequestrates CREB, CBP transcription factors).','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Neurodegenerative Diseases II','easy','mcq','Sporadic CJD classically presents with which triad of findings?','["A. Ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, confusion (Wernicke triad)","B. Rapidly progressive dementia, myoclonus, and periodic sharp wave complexes (PSWCs) on EEG","C. Chorea, psychiatric symptoms, and cognitive decline","D. Resting tremor, rigidity, and bradykinesia"]','B','Sporadic CJD: rapidly progressive dementia (weeks to months), myoclonus (stimulus-sensitive), cerebellar signs, visual disturbances. EEG: periodic sharp wave complexes (1-2 Hz, triphasic). CSF: elevated 14-3-3 protein and RT-QuIC positive (supersensitive prion diagnostic test). MRI DWI: cortical ribboning or basal ganglia hyperintensity. Median survival: 4-6 months from symptom onset. Invariably fatal.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Tumours I','easy','mcq','Glioblastoma (WHO grade 4 astrocytoma) is characterised on histology by which PATHOGNOMONIC finding?','["A. Psammoma bodies","B. Pseudopalisading necrosis (tumour cells arranged around zones of necrosis)","C. Homer-Wright rosettes","D. Perivascular pseudorosettes"]','B','Glioblastoma (GBM): pseudopalisading necrosis (tumour cells arranged in pseudopalissading pattern around central necrosis) and microvascular proliferation (glomeruloid tufts) are the WHO defining histological features. Also: nuclear pleomorphism, mitoses, EGFR amplification, PTEN loss, IDH-wildtype (in primary GBM). Most common primary malignant brain tumour in adults. Median survival: 14-16 months with standard treatment.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Tumours I','easy','mcq','Meningiomas characteristically arise from which cell type?','["A. Astrocytes","B. Ependymal cells","C. Arachnoid cap (meningothelial) cells","D. Schwann cells"]','C','Meningiomas arise from arachnoid cap cells (meningothelial cells of the arachnoid). They are typically extra-axial (outside the brain parenchyma), dura-attached, and benign (WHO grade 1 in ~80%). Classic locations: convexity, parasagittal, sphenoid wing, olfactory groove, posterior fossa. Association with NF2 (chromosome 22q12, merlin mutation). Psammoma bodies (calcified concentric lamellae) are characteristic.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Tumours I','medium','mcq','A 45-year-old presents with a slow-growing brain tumour. MRI shows a heterogeneous mass with calcification, low grade. Biopsy shows IDH1-mutant astrocytoma with ATRX loss and no EGFR amplification. This is most consistent with:','["A. WHO grade 4 glioblastoma (IDH-wildtype)","B. WHO grade 2 or 3 diffuse astrocytoma (IDH-mutant)","C. Oligodendroglioma (IDH-mutant, 1p/19q co-deleted)","D. Pilocytic astrocytoma (WHO grade 1)"]','B','Diffuse astrocytoma (IDH-mutant, WHO grade 2) or anaplastic astrocytoma (IDH-mutant, WHO grade 3): IDH mutation (IDH1 R132H in ~90%, IDH2 R172K in ~10%) PLUS ATRX loss (ATRX is an alternative lengthening of telomeres mechanism marker — distinguishes astrocytoma from oligodendroglioma which retains ATRX but has 1p/19q co-deletion). IDH-mutant tumours have better prognosis than IDH-wildtype GBM.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Tumours I','medium','mcq','Oligodendroglioma is characterised by which molecular markers in the 2021 WHO CNS tumour classification?','["A. IDH-wildtype and MGMT promoter methylation","B. IDH mutation AND 1p/19q chromosomal co-deletion (complete)","C. EGFR amplification and PTEN loss","D. BRAF V600E mutation and CDKN2A deletion"]','B','Oligodendroglioma (WHO grade 2 or 3) is molecularly defined by: IDH mutation (IDH1 or IDH2) AND complete 1p/19q co-deletion (loss of the short arm of chromosome 1 and the long arm of chromosome 19). The 1p/19q co-deletion results from an unbalanced translocation. Histology: fried egg cells (perinuclear halo artefact), chicken wire vasculature, calcification. Better prognosis and chemosensitive (PCV and temozolomide).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Tumours I','medium','mcq','MGMT promoter methylation in glioblastoma is clinically important because:','["A. It predicts resistance to temozolomide chemotherapy","B. It predicts BENEFIT from temozolomide alkylating chemotherapy (MGMT methylation silences the DNA repair enzyme that removes alkyl adducts)","C. It indicates IDH mutation","D. It is a marker of pseudopalisading necrosis"]','B','MGMT (O6-methylguanine-DNA methyltransferase): a DNA repair enzyme that removes alkyl adducts placed by temozolomide (TMZ). MGMT promoter methylation SILENCES MGMT expression → reduced repair of TMZ-induced DNA damage → greater tumour cell death. ~50% of GBM are MGMT-methylated → better response to TMZ (median survival ~21.7 months vs ~12.7 months in unmethylated). Standard GBM treatment: surgery + radiotherapy + TMZ (Stupp protocol).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Tumours I','medium','mcq','Psammoma bodies in meningioma represent which pathological structure?','["A. Extracellular amyloid deposits in the tumour stroma","B. Concentrically lamellated calcifications within whorled meningothelial cell nests","C. Haemosiderin-laden macrophages within the tumour","D. Eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusions in meningothelial cells"]','B','Psammoma bodies: concentrically lamellated calcifications (calcospherites) that form within whorled clusters of meningothelial cells in meningiomas. Also seen in: papillary thyroid carcinoma, papillary serous ovarian carcinoma, papillary mesothelioma. In meningioma, the meningothelial subtype (WHO grade 1) has the most psammoma bodies.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Tumours I','medium','mcq','Which clinical syndrome is associated with bilateral acoustic neuromas (vestibular schwannomas)?','["A. Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) — von Recklinghausen disease","B. Neurofibromatosis type 2 (NF2) — autosomal dominant, chromosome 22q12, merlin mutation","C. Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome","D. Tuberous sclerosis complex"]','B','NF2 (chromosome 22q12, NF2/merlin gene — a tumour suppressor): bilateral vestibular schwannomas (acoustic neuromas) are pathognomonic. Also: multiple meningiomas, spinal ependymomas, cataracts. Distinct from NF1 (chromosome 17q11, NF1/neurofibromin gene): cafe-au-lait spots, neurofibromas, optic gliomas, Lisch nodules in the iris, axillary/inguinal freckling, pseudarthrosis, malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumours.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Tumours I','hard','mcq','The IDH mutation in gliomas leads to production of which oncometabolite, and what is its effect?','["A. Succinate accumulation causing HIF-1alpha stabilisation and angiogenesis","B. 2-hydroxyglutarate (2-HG) causing histone and DNA hypermethylation (CpG island methylator phenotype, CIMP), impairing differentiation","C. Fumarate accumulation causing defective DNA repair","D. Pyruvate accumulation causing aerobic glycolysis (Warburg effect)"]','B','IDH1/IDH2 mutations cause neomorphic enzyme activity: instead of converting isocitrate to alpha-ketoglutarate (alpha-KG), mutant IDH converts alpha-KG to 2-hydroxyglutarate (2-HG). 2-HG inhibits alpha-KG-dependent dioxygenases (TET methylcytosine hydroxylases, histone demethylases), causing widespread epigenetic changes: CpG island methylator phenotype (CIMP), histone hypermethylation, and impaired cellular differentiation. IDH-mutant gliomas have better prognosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Tumours I','hard','mcq','Which immunohistochemical marker is used to distinguish astrocytoma from reactive gliosis in brain biopsy specimens?','["A. GFAP (glial fibrillary acidic protein)","B. IDH1 R132H immunohistochemistry (mutation-specific antibody) — positive in astrocytoma, negative in reactive gliosis","C. Ki-67 (MIB-1 proliferation index)","D. EMA (epithelial membrane antigen)"]','B','IDH1 R132H immunohistochemistry: a mutation-specific antibody that is POSITIVE in IDH1-mutant gliomas (astrocytoma, oligodendroglioma) and NEGATIVE in reactive gliosis and IDH-wildtype GBM. This is extremely useful in diagnostically challenging biopsies. GFAP marks astrocytic differentiation (but is also positive in reactive astrocytes). Ki-67 assesses proliferation but does not distinguish tumour from reactive.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Tumours I','easy','mcq','The most common primary brain tumour in adults overall is:','["A. Oligodendroglioma","B. Glioblastoma (GBM)","C. Meningioma","D. Primary CNS lymphoma"]','C','Meningioma is actually the MOST COMMON primary brain tumour in adults overall (~36-37% of primary brain tumours). However, among MALIGNANT primary brain tumours, glioblastoma is the most common. Meningiomas are predominantly benign (WHO grade 1, ~80%) and more common in women (2:1 ratio). They are often incidentally found on MRI.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Tumours II','easy','mcq','Medulloblastoma characteristically occurs in which location and age group?','["A. Adults, temporal lobe","B. Children, cerebellar vermis (posterior fossa)","C. Elderly, cerebral convexity","D. Adolescents, pineal region"]','B','Medulloblastoma: most common malignant brain tumour of childhood. Located in the cerebellar vermis (children) or lateral hemispheres (older patients, desmoplastic variant). WHO grade 4 (highly malignant). Presents: hydrocephalus (4th ventricle obstruction), truncal ataxia, vomiting. Spreads via CSF (drop metastases). Molecular subtypes: WNT-activated (best prognosis), SHH-activated, Group 3, Group 4.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Tumours II','easy','mcq','Craniopharyngioma arises from which embryological remnant?','["A. Rathke pouch (oral ectoderm/stomodeum) remnants in the sella/suprasellar region","B. Neural crest cells in the cavernous sinus","C. Endodermal remnants of the fourth ventricle","D. Notochordal remnants at the clivus"]','A','Craniopharyngioma arises from Rathke pouch remnants (oral ectoderm). Location: sellar/suprasellar (near the pituitary stalk and hypothalamus). Two types: adamantinomatous (children/young adults, calcification, machine oil cysts) and papillary (adults, WHO grade 1, no calcification). Clinically: visual field defects (optic chiasm compression), pituitary dysfunction, diabetes insipidus, hydrocephalus. Characteristic: calcification visible on CT.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Tumours II','medium','mcq','Ependymoma arises from which cells and what is the characteristic histological feature?','["A. Schwann cells in the posterior fossa; schwannoma histology","B. Ependymal cells lining the ventricles and central canal; characteristic perivascular pseudorosettes","C. Oligodendrocytes in the temporal lobe; fried egg cells","D. Arachnoid cap cells; whorls and psammoma bodies"]','B','Ependymoma: arises from ependymal cells lining the ventricles (4th ventricle most common in children) and central canal of the spinal cord (spinal ependymoma most common in adults). WHO grade 2 (classic) or 3 (anaplastic). Histological hallmarks: perivascular pseudorosettes (tumour cells arranged around blood vessels with anuclear fibrillary zones) and true ependymal rosettes (canal-like structures). IHC: GFAP+, EMA dot-like positivity.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Tumours II','medium','mcq','A 12-year-old presents with a calcified, cystic tumour in the posterior fossa with a mural nodule. MRI shows a cystic lesion with an enhancing nodule. Biopsy shows biphasic pattern with Rosenthal fibres and eosinophilic granular bodies. This is most consistent with:','["A. Medulloblastoma","B. Pilocytic astrocytoma (WHO grade 1)","C. Ependymoma","D. Craniopharyngioma"]','B','Pilocytic astrocytoma (WHO grade 1): most common brain tumour in children. Classic location: posterior fossa/cerebellum, optic pathway, hypothalamus. MRI: cystic lesion with enhancing mural nodule. Histology: biphasic pattern (loose and compact areas), Rosenthal fibres (elongated, corkscrew-shaped, eosinophilic protein aggregates in astrocyte processes), eosinophilic granular bodies. BRAF-KIAA1549 fusion in most cases. Excellent prognosis after resection.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Tumours II','medium','mcq','Acoustic neuroma (vestibular schwannoma) arises from which nerve component and at which location?','["A. Cochlear division of CN VIII in the internal auditory canal","B. Vestibular division of CN VIII (superior or inferior vestibular nerve) at the transition zone (Obersteiner-Redlich zone) in the internal auditory canal/CPA","C. Facial nerve (CN VII) within the parotid gland","D. Trigeminal nerve (CN V) in the Meckel cave"]','B','Vestibular schwannoma arises from Schwann cells of the VESTIBULAR branch (superior or inferior) of CN VIII, at the Obersteiner-Redlich zone (transition between central myelin from oligodendrocytes and peripheral myelin from Schwann cells), located in the internal auditory canal extending to the cerebellopontine angle (CPA). Despite the name acoustic neuroma, most arise from vestibular nerve. Presents: unilateral sensorineural hearing loss, tinnitus, disequilibrium.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Tumours II','medium','mcq','Primary CNS lymphoma (PCNSL) in immunocompetent patients is predominantly which histological subtype?','["A. T-cell lymphoma","B. Hodgkin lymphoma","C. Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL)","D. Marginal zone B-cell lymphoma"]','C','PCNSL is almost universally diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) in immunocompetent patients. In immunocompromised patients (HIV, post-transplant), it is EBV-driven DLBCL. Location: periventricular white matter, corpus callosum, basal ganglia. MRI: homogeneously enhancing mass(es) — in contrast to GBM which heterogeneously enhances. Responds dramatically to methotrexate-based chemotherapy (but steroids can make it transiently disappear on imaging — steroid effect).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Tumours II','medium','mcq','Which tumour is associated with the RB1 gene mutation and Von Hippel-Lindau (VHL) disease causes cerebellar haemangioblastoma in addition to which other lesions?','["A. Retinoblastoma (RB1) and VHL syndrome (haemangioblastoma + renal cell carcinoma + phaeochromocytoma + retinal angioma)","B. Medulloblastoma (RB1) and VHL syndrome (haemangioblastoma + acoustic neuroma)","C. Glioblastoma (RB1) and VHL syndrome (haemangioblastoma + craniopharyngioma)","D. Ependymoma (RB1) and VHL syndrome (haemangioblastoma + meningioma)"]','A','Retinoblastoma: caused by RB1 tumour suppressor gene mutations (chromosome 13q14), paradigm of the two-hit hypothesis. VHL disease (chromosome 3p25, VHL tumour suppressor gene): autosomal dominant. Cerebellar and retinal haemangioblastomas, clear cell renal cell carcinoma (bilateral/multifocal), phaeochromocytoma, pancreatic cysts and tumours, endolymphatic sac tumours. HIF-1alpha pathway dysregulation due to VHL loss.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Tumours II','hard','mcq','Tuberous sclerosis complex (TSC) can cause which CNS tumour that is a benign, slow-growing tumour near the foramen of Monro causing obstructive hydrocephalus?','["A. Subependymal giant cell astrocytoma (SEGA)","B. Medulloblastoma","C. Craniopharyngioma","D. Ependymoma"]','A','TSC (mutations in TSC1 [hamartin] or TSC2 [tuberin], mTOR pathway tumour suppressors): causes SEGA — subependymal giant cell astrocytoma — a slow-growing, benign tumour (WHO grade 1) arising from subependymal nodules near the foramen of Monro. If it obstructs CSF flow → hydrocephalus. Treatment: surgical resection or mTOR inhibitor (everolimus). TSC also causes: cortical tubers (epilepsy), retinal hamartomas, renal angiomyolipomas, pulmonary lymphangioleiomyomatosis, cardiac rhabdomyomas.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Tumours II','hard','mcq','The molecular subgrouping of medulloblastoma has major prognostic implications. Which subgroup has the BEST prognosis?','["A. Group 3 (MYC amplified)","B. Group 4 (most common)","C. SHH-activated (TP53-mutant)","D. WNT-activated (beta-catenin nuclear)"]','D','WNT-activated medulloblastoma: ~10% of cases, activating mutations in CTNNB1 (beta-catenin) with nuclear accumulation. Located in the midline/4th ventricle, predominantly in children/adolescents. BEST PROGNOSIS: >95% 5-year survival. Histology: classic histology. Biomarker: nuclear beta-catenin on IHC. Group 3 (MYC amplification, 5-year OS ~50%) and SHH-TP53 mutant (5-year OS ~40%) have the worst prognosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','CNS Tumours II','easy','mcq','The most common location for an ependymoma in ADULTS is:','["A. Fourth ventricle","B. Lateral ventricles","C. Spinal cord (especially lumbar, myxopapillary ependymoma)","D. Cerebral hemispheres"]','C','In adults, ependymoma is MOST commonly spinal, particularly myxopapillary ependymoma (WHO grade 2) in the lumbar spinal canal/conus medullaris/filum terminale. Myxopapillary ependymoma: papillary architecture with mucoid (myxoid) stroma. In children, 4th ventricle is the most common site. Spinal ependymomas have an excellent prognosis after gross total resection.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Peripheral Neuropathies','easy','mcq','Guillain-Barre syndrome (GBS) is characterised on CSF analysis by:','["A. Neutrophilic pleocytosis with low glucose","B. Albuminocytologic dissociation (elevated protein with normal or minimal pleocytosis)","C. Lymphocytic pleocytosis with xanthochromia","D. Normal CSF in all cases"]','B','GBS (acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy, AIDP): CSF shows albuminocytologic dissociation — HIGH PROTEIN (>0.45 g/L, due to nerve root inflammation and breakdown of the blood-nerve barrier) with NORMAL or near-normal WBC count. This combination is characteristic. Protein peaks at 4-6 weeks. Note: early in the disease, CSF may be normal. Normal opening pressure.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Peripheral Neuropathies','easy','mcq','Guillain-Barre syndrome most commonly follows which type of preceding infection?','["A. Influenza A infection","B. Campylobacter jejuni gastroenteritis (most common), also CMV, EBV, HIV, Zika virus","C. Streptococcal pharyngitis","D. Hepatitis B vaccination"]','B','GBS is an autoimmune polyneuropathy typically triggered 1-4 weeks after infection (molecular mimicry between pathogen antigens and peripheral nerve antigens). Campylobacter jejuni is the most common preceding infection (~30% of cases, especially the axonal variants AMAN/AMSAN). Other triggers: CMV, EBV, HIV, Zika, influenza, and rarely vaccines (influenza 1976 swine flu vaccine had increased risk).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Peripheral Neuropathies','medium','mcq','Charcot-Marie-Tooth (CMT) disease type 1A, the most common inherited peripheral neuropathy, is caused by:','["A. Point mutation in the MPZ (myelin protein zero) gene","B. Duplication of chromosome 17p11.2 containing the PMP22 (peripheral myelin protein 22) gene","C. Trinucleotide repeat expansion in the DMPK gene","D. Loss of function mutation in the connexin 32 (GJB1) gene"]','B','CMT1A: autosomal dominant, duplication of a 1.4 Mb region on chromosome 17p11.2 containing the PMP22 gene. PMP22 overdose causes dysfunctional myelin compaction → hypertrophic demyelinating neuropathy (onion bulb formation: concentric Schwann cell lamellae around an axon). NCS: severely reduced conduction velocity. CMT1B: MPZ mutation (myelin protein zero). CMTX1: connexin 32/GJB1 mutation (X-linked).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Peripheral Neuropathies','medium','mcq','Diabetic peripheral neuropathy most commonly presents as which pattern?','["A. Proximal motor-predominant neuropathy affecting the shoulders","B. Length-dependent distal symmetrical sensorimotor polyneuropathy (glove-and-stocking pattern)","C. Multifocal motor neuropathy with conduction block","D. Mononeuritis multiplex predominantly affecting cranial nerves"]','B','Diabetic peripheral neuropathy (most common form): length-dependent distal symmetrical sensorimotor polyneuropathy (glove-and-stocking distribution). Mechanism: hyperglycaemia causes polyol pathway activation, advanced glycation end-products (AGEs), oxidative stress, and endoneurial microangiopathy → axonal degeneration (dying-back axonopathy) with secondary demyelination. Presents: distal tingling, numbness, burning, with absent ankle reflexes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Peripheral Neuropathies','medium','mcq','Onion bulb formation on nerve biopsy is characteristic of which type of neuropathy?','["A. Axonal neuropathy (Wallerian degeneration pattern)","B. Chronic demyelinating neuropathy with repeated demyelination/remyelination cycles (e.g., CMT1, CIDP)","C. Vasculitic neuropathy","D. Amyloid neuropathy"]','B','Onion bulb formation: concentric layers of supernumerary Schwann cell processes (and fibroblast-like cells) arranged around a central axon — like layers of an onion. Caused by repeated cycles of demyelination and remyelination over many years, with each remyelination cycle adding a new layer. Classic in: CMT1 (hereditary demyelinating neuropathy), CIDP (chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Peripheral Neuropathies','medium','mcq','A patient develops ascending flaccid paralysis with absent reflexes, autonomic instability, and respiratory failure 2 weeks after Campylobacter gastroenteritis. Which variant of GBS is associated with anti-GM1 antibodies?','["A. Acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (AIDP)","B. Acute motor axonal neuropathy (AMAN) — associated with anti-GM1/GD1a IgG antibodies (molecular mimicry with C. jejuni lipopolysaccharide)","C. Miller Fisher syndrome","D. Acute motor and sensory axonal neuropathy (AMSAN)"]','B','AMAN: a pure motor GBS variant (axonal, not demyelinating), common in East Asia, strongly associated with Campylobacter jejuni and anti-GM1/GD1a antibodies. Anti-GQ1b IgG antibodies are associated with Miller Fisher syndrome (MFS): ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, areflexia triad — a GBS variant with predominant cranial nerve involvement. AMAN is diagnosed by NCS: normal conduction velocity, reduced CMAP amplitudes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Peripheral Neuropathies','medium','mcq','The clinical features of GBS include which of the following in the typical progression?','["A. Ascending symmetrical weakness starting distally, with areflexia, peak deficit at 2-4 weeks","B. Descending paralysis starting with cranial nerves and descending","C. Progressive proximal weakness with preserved reflexes","D. Rapid progression over hours to complete paralysis"]','A','Classic GBS: ascending symmetrical weakness starting distally (legs) and ascending over days-weeks, with AREFLEXIA (often early sign). Cranial nerve involvement (bilateral facial palsy, CN IX/X causing dysphagia). Autonomic dysfunction (cardiac arrhythmias, BP lability). Peak deficit at 2-4 weeks. Respiratory failure in ~25-30% (monitor FVC). Treatment: IVIG or plasma exchange. Most recover, 5% mortality.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Peripheral Neuropathies','hard','mcq','In hereditary motor and sensory neuropathy (HMSN/CMT), nerve conduction studies (NCS) distinguish demyelinating from axonal forms. Which NCS finding indicates a primary demyelinating neuropathy?','["A. Normal conduction velocity with reduced CMAP amplitude","B. Uniformly slow conduction velocity (<38 m/s in the upper limbs) with conduction block","C. Reduced SNAP amplitudes with normal conduction velocity","D. Absent F-waves with normal conduction velocity"]','B','Demyelinating CMT (e.g., CMT1): UNIFORMLY slow conduction velocities (<38 m/s median nerve, upper limbs) WITHOUT conduction block. The slowing is uniform across all nerves (in contrast to acquired demyelinating neuropathies like CIDP/GBS which show multifocal conduction block and temporal dispersion). Axonal CMT (CMT2): normal or near-normal conduction velocity with reduced CMAP/SNAP amplitudes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Peripheral Neuropathies','hard','mcq','The pathophysiology of diabetic autonomic neuropathy includes involvement of which structures, and what are its clinical manifestations?','["A. Motor neuron loss causing proximal muscle weakness and atrophy","B. Loss of small C-fibres and unmyelinated autonomic fibres causing orthostatic hypotension, gastroparesis, sexual dysfunction, neurogenic bladder, and impaired sudomotor function","C. Demyelination of sensory pathways causing a glove-and-stocking sensory loss","D. Microinfarction of nerve trunks causing mononeuritis multiplex"]','B','Diabetic autonomic neuropathy affects: cardiovascular (orthostatic hypotension, resting tachycardia, exercise intolerance, impaired heart rate variability, sudden death risk), GI (gastroparesis, diabetic diarrhoea, constipation), genitourinary (erectile dysfunction, retrograde ejaculation, neurogenic bladder — overflow incontinence), sudomotor (anhidrosis distally, gustatory sweating). Driven by loss of small C-fibres (pain/temperature/autonomic) preceding large fibre loss.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Peripheral Neuropathies','easy','mcq','The most feared complication of GBS requiring monitoring and intervention is:','["A. Permanent limb paralysis","B. Respiratory failure due to diaphragm and intercostal muscle weakness","C. Cardiac arrest due to hyperkalemia","D. Status epilepticus"]','B','Respiratory failure is the most feared acute complication of GBS, occurring in ~25-30% of patients. The diaphragm and intercostal muscles become weak as the ascending paralysis progresses. Monitoring: serial FVC measurements (intubate if FVC <20 mL/kg, or 20-30 rule: <20 mL/kg FVC, <30 cmH2O MIP, <40 cmH2O MEP). ICU admission for close monitoring is essential in rapidly progressive GBS.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Epilepsy Pathology','easy','mcq','Hippocampal sclerosis (Ammon horn sclerosis) is the most common pathological finding in which type of epilepsy?','["A. Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy (JME)","B. Mesial temporal lobe epilepsy (MTLE)","C. Lennox-Gastaut syndrome","D. West syndrome (infantile spasms)"]','B','Hippocampal sclerosis (HS) is the hallmark of mesial temporal lobe epilepsy (MTLE): neuronal loss (predominantly CA1 > CA4 > CA3, with relative sparing of CA2 and dentate gyrus granule cells) and gliosis in the hippocampus. MRI: hippocampal atrophy and T2/FLAIR signal increase. HS may be caused by prolonged febrile seizures in childhood (dual pathology). Surgical resection of the sclerotic hippocampus achieves seizure freedom in ~60-70% of carefully selected patients.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Epilepsy Pathology','easy','mcq','Focal cortical dysplasia (FCD) is an important cause of drug-resistant epilepsy. Which HISTOLOGICAL feature is specific for FCD type IIb (Taylor type)?','["A. Granule cell dispersion in the dentate gyrus","B. Balloon cells (large dysmorphic neurons with cytoplasmic accumulation of neurofilaments)","C. Cortical laminar necrosis with pyramidal cell loss","D. Micropolygyria"]','B','FCD type IIb (Taylor-type): histological hallmarks are dysmorphic neurons (large, misshapen neurons with abnormal orientation and cytoplasmic neurofilament accumulation) AND balloon cells (large cells with abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm and eccentrically placed nuclei, resembling tuberous sclerosis tubers). Balloon cells are SPECIFIC for FCD IIb. Often in the temporal lobe. mTOR pathway activation is the underlying molecular mechanism (same as TSC).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Epilepsy Pathology','medium','mcq','Which genetic mutation is the most common cause of focal cortical dysplasia types II and III?','["A. TSC1/TSC2 mutations (mTOR pathway upstream regulators)","B. Somatic mutations in MTOR, PIK3CA, and other mTOR pathway genes (mTORopathies)","C. CDKL5 mutations","D. SCN1A mutations"]','B','FCD types IIa and IIb are caused by somatic (post-zygotic, second hit) mutations in mTOR pathway genes: MTOR itself (most common), PIK3CA, PIK3R2, DEPDC5, TSC1/TSC2. These result in focal mTOR hyperactivation in a subset of cortical cells during development → abnormal neuronal migration/organisation. Because somatic (not germline), standard blood DNA testing is often negative; requires brain tissue or high-sensitivity sequencing.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Epilepsy Pathology','medium','mcq','Which pattern of neuronal loss in hippocampal sclerosis (HS) is most closely associated with mesial TLE?','["A. Total neuronal loss of all hippocampal sectors equally","B. Predominant loss in CA1 (Sommer sector) and CA4 (end folium), with relative sparing of CA2","C. Predominant loss in CA3 and dentate gyrus only","D. Loss only in the subiculum"]','B','Classic hippocampal sclerosis (HS type 1, ILAE classification): severe loss predominantly in CA1 and CA4 (end folium), less severe in CA3, relative sparing of CA2. CA2 neurons are relatively resistant to ischaemic/epileptic injury. HS type 2: predominant CA1 loss. HS type 3: predominant CA4 loss. Granule cell dispersion (widening of the dentate gyrus granule cell layer) is an associated feature.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Epilepsy Pathology','medium','mcq','Which imaging finding on MRI is MOST sensitive for detecting hippocampal sclerosis in MTLE?','["A. Gadolinium enhancement of the hippocampus","B. T2/FLAIR hyperintensity and hippocampal volume loss (atrophy) on coronal T2-weighted and FLAIR sequences","C. Diffusion restriction in the hippocampus","D. Cortical thinning visible on volumetric T1 images only"]','B','MRI hippocampal sclerosis: T2/FLAIR hyperintensity (gliosis replaces lost neurons → increased water content → T2 signal increase) AND hippocampal atrophy (volume loss compared to the contralateral side) on thin coronal slices perpendicular to the long axis of the hippocampus. High-resolution 3T MRI protocols and volumetric analysis increase sensitivity. Also: loss of internal hippocampal architecture, atrophy of ipsilateral fornix/mammillary body.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Epilepsy Pathology','medium','mcq','Cortical dysplasias are classified by the 2022 ILAE classification into types I, II, and III. FCD type III is defined as:','["A. Isolated FCD without an adjacent lesion","B. FCD associated with a principal lesion (hippocampal sclerosis type IIIa, tumour type IIIb, vascular malformation type IIIc, or acquired lesion type IIId)","C. FCD with balloon cells and dysmorphic neurons (Taylor type)","D. FCD with abnormal cortical lamination only, without dysmorphic neurons"]','B','FCD type III (ILAE 2011/2022): FCD adjacent to or associated with a PRINCIPAL LESION: IIIa = FCD + hippocampal sclerosis (most common type III); IIIb = FCD + tumour (ganglioglioma, DNET); IIIc = FCD + vascular malformation (cavernoma, AVM); IIId = FCD + acquired lesion from early life (infarct, trauma, encephalitis). The FCD is likely secondary to the principal lesion or shares a common aetiology.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Epilepsy Pathology','medium','mcq','Tuberous sclerosis (TSC) causes epilepsy via cortical tubers. What is the histological composition of a cortical tuber?','["A. Normal cortical neurons in abnormal laminar organisation","B. Dysmorphic neurons, giant cells (balloon-cell like), abnormal astrocytes, loss of cortical lamination, and abundant reactive gliosis","C. Amyloid plaque deposits in the cortex","D. Microangiopathic changes with cortical microinfarcts"]','B','TSC cortical tubers: cortical lesions with complete disruption of normal cortical lamination. Contains: giant cells (large cells resembling balloon cells of FCD IIb — with both neuronal and glial markers: NeuN+, GFAP+), dysmorphic neurons, abundant astrocytes, and reactive gliosis. The giant cells accumulate phosphorylated S6 kinase (mTORC1 pathway hyperactivation). Epileptogenicity may arise from both the tubers and the surrounding cortex.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Epilepsy Pathology','hard','mcq','The mechanisms by which hippocampal sclerosis causes seizures in MTLE include which of the following?','["A. Loss of inhibitory interneurons (GABAergic) in the hippocampus causing hyperexcitability, sprouting of mossy fibres forming recurrent excitatory circuits onto granule cells, and astrocytic K+ buffering failure","B. Loss of CA1 pyramidal neurons causing reduced hippocampal output only","C. Hypermyelination of hippocampal pathways reducing signal conduction velocity","D. Overexpression of NMDA receptors in the dentate gyrus"]','A','MTLE pathophysiology: (1) Loss of inhibitory interneurons (especially parvalbumin+ and somatostatin+ interneurons in the hilus/CA1) reduces GABA-mediated inhibition → net excitation. (2) Mossy fibre sprouting (axonal sprouting from surviving dentate granule cells into the inner molecular layer) creates recurrent excitatory circuits. (3) Reactive astrocytes lose K+ buffering (Kir4.1 channels) → extracellular K+ accumulation → neuronal hyperexcitability. (4) Gap junction coupling between astrocytes may spread excitation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Epilepsy Pathology','hard','mcq','The surgical pathology of cortical tubers in tuberous sclerosis complex differs from focal cortical dysplasia type IIb in which molecular characteristic?','["A. TSC cortical tubers have germline TSC1/TSC2 mutations (both alleles affected in tuber cells via second somatic hit), whereas FCD IIb has somatic MTOR pathway mutations only in the dysplastic cortex","B. TSC tubers contain amyloid deposits whereas FCD IIb does not","C. FCD IIb has germline TP53 mutations whereas TSC is sporadic","D. TSC tubers affect grey matter only while FCD IIb extends into white matter"]','A','Both TSC tubers and FCD IIb involve mTOR pathway hyperactivation and have nearly identical histology (balloon cells, dysmorphic neurons). The distinction: TSC = germline mutation in TSC1 or TSC2 (first hit) + somatic second hit in the tuber cells (two-hit mechanism). FCD IIb = somatic (post-zygotic) mutations in MTOR, PIK3CA, or other mTOR pathway genes — germline normal. Clinically: TSC is a multisystem disorder; FCD IIb is isolated brain involvement.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Epilepsy Pathology','easy','mcq','Drug-resistant epilepsy is defined as failure of adequate trials of how many appropriately chosen anti-seizure medications (ASMs)?','["A. One ASM","B. Two ASMs","C. Three ASMs","D. Four or more ASMs"]','B','Drug-resistant (pharmacoresistant) epilepsy: failure of ADEQUATE TRIALS of TWO or more appropriately chosen, adequately dosed ASMs (either as monotherapy or in combination) — ILAE 2010 definition. After failure of 2 ASMs, the probability of achieving seizure freedom with further ASMs is <5%. These patients should be referred to epilepsy surgery programmes for evaluation. Common causes: hippocampal sclerosis, FCD, tumours (DNET, ganglioglioma).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Raised ICP & Herniation','easy','mcq','Cushing triad, a clinical sign of severely raised ICP, consists of which three findings?','["A. Fever, tachycardia, and hypotension","B. Hypertension (widened pulse pressure), bradycardia, and irregular/slow respirations","C. Hypotension, tachycardia, and hyperpyrexia","D. Hypertension, tachycardia, and Cheyne-Stokes breathing"]','B','Cushing triad (Cushing response): hypertension (widened pulse pressure, systolic hypertension from sympathetic surge responding to brainstem ischaemia) + bradycardia (vagal reflex in response to severe hypertension) + irregular/slow respirations (medullary respiratory centre ischaemia). This is a PRE-TERMINAL sign of severely raised ICP and impending herniation. Requires IMMEDIATE intervention.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Raised ICP & Herniation','easy','mcq','Transtentorial (uncal) herniation occurs when which structure herniates through the tentorial notch?','["A. Cingulate gyrus under the falx cerebri","B. Medial temporal lobe (uncus and parahippocampal gyrus) through the tentorial incisura","C. Cerebellar tonsils through the foramen magnum","D. Midbrain through the tentorial notch"]','B','Uncal herniation: the uncus and parahippocampal gyrus (medial temporal lobe) are pushed through the tentorial incisura (the opening in the tentorium cerebelli) by a supratentorial mass. This compresses: CN III (ipsilateral fixed dilated pupil — the first sign), PCA (contralateral occipital infarction), and midbrain (Duret haemorrhages — pontine haemorrhages due to tearing of perforating vessels).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Raised ICP & Herniation','medium','mcq','Which type of cerebral oedema is caused by disruption of the blood-brain barrier, allowing protein-rich fluid to enter the interstitium?','["A. Cytotoxic oedema","B. Vasogenic oedema","C. Osmotic oedema","D. Interstitial (transependymal) oedema"]','B','Vasogenic oedema: BBB disruption → protein-rich fluid enters the brain extracellular space. Predominantly in WHITE MATTER (higher compliance). Causes: tumour, abscess, contusion, haemorrhage, hypertensive encephalopathy. MRI: T2/FLAIR bright in white matter (finger-like projections). Treatment: dexamethasone (reduces peritumoral vasogenic oedema). Cytotoxic oedema: intracellular swelling due to Na+/K+ ATPase failure (ischaemia). Interstitial: hydrocephalus with transependymal CSF seepage.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Raised ICP & Herniation','medium','mcq','Subfalcine (cingulate) herniation occurs when the cingulate gyrus herniates under the falx cerebri. Which artery can be compressed, causing which syndrome?','["A. Middle cerebral artery causing contralateral face and arm weakness","B. Anterior cerebral artery (ACA) causing contralateral leg weakness","C. Posterior cerebral artery causing occipital lobe infarction","D. Anterior choroidal artery causing contralateral hemiplegia"]','B','Subfalcine herniation: cingulate gyrus herniates under the falx cerebri (horizontally). The ACA, which runs in the interhemispheric fissure along the medial surface, can be compressed → contralateral leg weakness (ACA territory supplies the medial frontal and parietal cortex representing the lower limb). May be bilateral if both ACAs are compressed by the herniating tissue.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Raised ICP & Herniation','medium','mcq','Tonsillar herniation (cerebellar tonsillar herniation through the foramen magnum) causes death by which mechanism?','["A. Compression of the basilar artery causing pontine infarction","B. Compression of the medulla oblongata causing cardiorespiratory arrest (respiratory centre failure, vasomotor centre failure)","C. Upward herniation of the cerebellum compressing the midbrain","D. Compression of CN IX and X causing dysphagia and aspiration"]','B','Tonsillar herniation: cerebellar tonsils are pushed through the foramen magnum by raised infratentorial ICP (posterior fossa mass) or severe global ICP. This compresses the MEDULLA OBLONGATA (contains the cardiorespiratory centres: pre-Botzinger complex for respiration, vasomotor centre), causing respiratory arrest and cardiovascular collapse → death. This is why LP is CONTRAINDICATED in raised ICP (risk of herniation).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Raised ICP & Herniation','medium','mcq','A patient with known glioblastoma suddenly develops ipsilateral fixed dilated pupil and contralateral hemiplegia. The first priority in management is:','["A. Emergency LP to diagnose leptomeningeal spread","B. Emergency CT head to identify herniation/haemorrhage, then immediate medical management (mannitol, hyperventilation, neurosurgical consultation)","C. Urgent MRI brain with gadolinium to assess tumour progression","D. Blood cultures and empirical antibiotics for brain abscess"]','B','Fixed dilated pupil + contralateral hemiplegia = uncal herniation (CN III compression + cerebral peduncle compression). This is a NEUROSURGICAL EMERGENCY. Immediate management: 1) Emergency CT head (no LP), 2) Head elevation 30 degrees, 3) Mannitol 0.25-1 g/kg IV bolus (osmotherapy), 4) Controlled hyperventilation (target PCO2 30-35 mmHg) for acute ICP reduction, 5) Urgent neurosurgical consultation for decompression.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Raised ICP & Herniation','medium','mcq','Which formula expresses cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) and what is the minimum CPP required to maintain adequate cerebral blood flow?','["A. CPP = MAP + ICP; minimum 20 mmHg","B. CPP = MAP - ICP; minimum 50-60 mmHg","C. CPP = MAP x ICP; minimum 100 mmHg","D. CPP = SBP - ICP; minimum 30 mmHg"]','B','CPP = MAP (mean arterial pressure) - ICP. Normal CPP: 70-80 mmHg. Minimum to maintain cerebral blood flow: 50-60 mmHg (international guidelines often target >60 mmHg in TBI patients). If ICP equals MAP, CPP = 0 → no cerebral perfusion = brain death. In TBI management: target ICP <20 mmHg AND CPP 60-70 mmHg.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Raised ICP & Herniation','hard','mcq','Duret haemorrhages are seen in the brainstem as a complication of which type of herniation?','["A. Tonsillar herniation compressing the medulla","B. Transtentorial (uncal) herniation causing downward displacement of the brainstem, stretching and tearing small perforating arteries in the pons and midbrain","C. Subfalcine herniation compressing the ACA","D. Upward transtentorial herniation of the cerebellum"]','B','Duret haemorrhages: secondary haemorrhages in the PONS and upper midbrain, caused by downward displacement of the brainstem during transtentorial herniation. The perforating arteries (basilar perforators) are torn as the brainstem shifts caudally. Pathological: multiple small haemorrhages in the central pons/midbrain. These are NOT the primary lesion but a consequence of herniation. They indicate a catastrophic, often fatal, herniation event.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Raised ICP & Herniation','hard','mcq','The ICP waveform shows three components (P1, P2, P3). Raised ICP is characterised by which change in these components?','["A. P1 (percussion wave) becomes larger than P2 (tidal wave) and P3 (dicrotic wave)","B. P2 (tidal wave) becomes equal to or larger than P1, indicating reduced brain compliance (Lundberg A waves if ICP plateau waves develop)","C. P3 disappears entirely while P1 and P2 remain unchanged","D. All three waves increase proportionally without change in relative amplitudes"]','B','Normal ICP waveform: P1 (percussion wave, arterial pulsation) > P2 (tidal wave, brain compliance) > P3 (dicrotic notch, venous). When brain compliance is reduced (raised ICP), P2 rises to equal or exceed P1 — the amplitude of P2 reflects brain compliance. Lundberg A waves (plateau waves, 50-100 mmHg for 5-20 minutes): indicate severely impaired compliance, often associated with herniation. B waves (20-50 mmHg, 0.5-2/min): sleep-related, less dangerous.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pathology','Raised ICP & Herniation','easy','mcq','Normal pressure hydrocephalus (NPH) presents with which classic triad?','["A. Fever, headache, and photophobia","B. Gait apraxia (magnetic gait), urinary incontinence, and dementia (Hakim-Adams triad)","C. Papilloedema, headache, and visual obscurations","D. Nausea, vomiting, and cranial nerve palsies"]','B','Normal pressure hydrocephalus (NPH): communicating hydrocephalus with normal CSF opening pressure on LP (or low-normal). Hakim-Adams triad: (1) Gait apraxia (magnetic gait — wide-based, shuffling, with foot-floor adhesion), (2) Urinary incontinence, (3) Dementia (frontal-subcortical pattern). Gait is the most prominent and responds best to CSF shunting. Imaging: enlarged ventricles out of proportion to sulcal atrophy. A potentially reversible cause of dementia.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');